      "role": "user",
      "content": "Can you explain how the harmonic series affects the timbre of a flute, specifically regarding why it sounds different from a brass instrument even when playing the same fundamental frequency?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The harmonic series, the set of overtones that accompany the fundamental frequency, is what gives each instrument its unique *timbre*.  When you play the same fundamental note on a flute and a brass instrument, the flute's sound is rich with higher harmonics that are often more evenly distributed or possess a different spectral balance than those produced by the brass instrument. This difference in the relative strength and presence of those overtones is what your ear perceives as the distinct sonic character, or timbre.",
